<section id="s-instability" data-code="intro">
<div class="section-no">01 — Gradient Outliers</div>
<h2>The Instability of Token-Level On-Policy Distillation</h2>
<p>To construct resource-efficient **Small Reasoning Models (SRMs)**, developers often turn to **Knowledge Distillation**. Conventional sequence-level SFT (teacher-forcing) suffers from **exposure bias**, particularly in long-chain-of-thought generations. The student model only observes correct teacher prefixes during training, but at test time, its own errors propagate, causing it to quickly wander off-track.</p>

<p>**On-Policy Distillation (OPD)** resolves exposure bias by forcing the student to train on its own generated trajectories. Under the Reverse KL objective, the student generates sequences x ~ π<sub>S</sub> and receives token-level supervision from the teacher model π<sub>T</sub>. Because reasoning models generate extremely long output chains, full-vocabulary KL computation is memory-prohibitive. Thus, practitioners use the token-level K<sub>1</sub> estimator to approximate the gradient:</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Reverse KL Objective & K1 Gradient</div>
  J<sub>KD</sub> = -D<sub>KL</sub>(π<sub>S</sub> ‖ π<sub>T</sub>) = -E<sub>x∼π<sub>S</sub></sub> [log(π<sub>S</sub>(x) / π<sub>T</sub>(x))] <br>
  ∇J<sub>KD</sub> = E<sub>x∼π<sub>S</sub></sub> [∇<sub>θ</sub> log π<sub>S</sub>(x) · log(π<sub>T</sub>(x) / π<sub>S</sub>(x))]
</div>

<p>However, the K<sub>1</sub> gradient estimator has a severe vulnerability. When the student samples a low-quality token that the teacher strongly dislikes (P<sub>T</sub> ≪ P<sub>S</sub>), the token advantage term log(π<sub>T</sub>(x) / π<sub>S</sub>(x)) approaches -∞. These **unreliable policy-gradient outliers** propagate back through the model, destabilizing optimization and causing training collapse.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">gradient-instability-simulation.canvas</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">interactive gradient norm & entropy tracker</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="chart-controls">
      <button class="chart-btn active" id="sim-opd-btn">Vanilla OPD</button>
      <button class="chart-btn" id="sim-clip-btn">Clip Outlier</button>
      <button class="chart-btn" id="sim-tropd-btn">TrOPD (Proposed)</button>
      <button class="chart-btn" id="sim-run-btn" style="border-color: var(--cyan); color: var(--cyan); margin-left: auto;">Play Simulation</button>
    </div>
    <canvas id="grad-sim-canvas" style="width: 100%; height: 260px; background: #0b0b0e; border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 6px;"></canvas>
  </div>
</div>

<p>As simulated above, under **Vanilla OPD**, a single outlier token update causes a massive gradient spike, driving Policy Entropy to zero (collapse). **Clip Outlier** (used in REOPOLD) clips the advantage, but this removes valid learning signals. **TrOPD** stabilizes training by partitioning tokens and applying targeted estimators, keeping gradients small and preserving policy entropy for stable exploration.</p>
</section>

<section id="s-trust" data-code="trust_region">
<div class="section-no">02 — Trust Region</div>
<h2>Decoding Agreement & Adaptive Trust Regions</h2>
<p>To isolate outlier gradients without discarding valuable supervision, TrOPD dynamically classifies each student-generated token into either a **trust region** or an **outlier region**. Unlike heuristic-based filtering (such as only training on the top 20% high-entropy tokens, which degrades overall reasoning), TrOPD defines the trust region using the **decoding agreement ratio** between the student and teacher policies.</p>

<p>For each token sampled from the student policy, x ~ π<sub>S</sub>, the probability of registering it in the trust region (M<sub>x</sub> = 1) is sampled from a Bernoulli distribution:</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Trust Region Mask Probability</div>
  M<sub>x</sub> ~ Bernoulli(P<sub>trust</sub>(x)), &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; P<sub>trust</sub>(x) = min(π<sub>T</sub>(x) / π<sub>S</sub>(x), 1.0)
</div>

<p>This design is mathematically inspired by **speculative decoding**. In speculative decoding, a teacher verifier accepts a draft token proposed by a student with probability P<sub>accept</sub> = min(P<sub>T</sub>(x)/P<sub>S</sub>(x), 1.0). By filtering out tokens rejected under this criteria (M<sub>x</sub> = 0), TrOPD ensures that token-level reverse KL gradients are only computed where the student and teacher distributions are aligned.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">speculative-agreement-inspector</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click tokens to check agreement and trust region classification</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div style="font-size: 0.72rem; color: var(--muted); margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">Example math response: Solve $x^2 - 5x + 6 = 0$</div>
