<p>By using this adaptive trust region mask M<sub>x</sub>, TrOPD eliminates the need for manual hyperparameter tuning (like REOPOLD's clipping threshold τ), and automatically adjusts to the learning phase. As SFT progresses, student behavior mimics the teacher more closely, naturally expanding the trust region.</p>
</section>

<section id="s-outliers" data-code="outlier_fkd">
<div class="section-no">03 — Outlier Estimation</div>
<h2>Top-k Forward KL for Outlier Regions</h2>
<p>While masking outlier tokens (M<sub>x</sub> = 0) prevents gradient explosion, completely discarding outliers is highly inefficient. Outlier tokens represent regions of severe distribution mismatch, which are precisely the areas where the student is most prone to error and needs instruction the most. Simply ignoring them leaves a blind spot in the student's training.</p>

<p>TrOPD recovers this lost supervisory signal by switching the optimization perspective for outlier tokens. Instead of calculating Reverse KL (which is mode-seeking and evaluated on student samples), TrOPD applies an auxiliary **Top-k Forward KL (FKD)** objective. Because the expectation is taken over the teacher's distribution, it remains stable even when student probabilities are very small:</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Outlier Auxiliary Loss & Gradient</div>
  J<sub>x</sub><sup>FKL</sup> = -(1 - M<sub>x</sub>) · Σ<sub>v∈V<sub>k</sub><sup>T</sup></sub> π<sub>T</sub>(v) · log(π<sub>T</sub>(v) / π<sub>S</sub>(v)) <br>
  ∇J<sub>x</sub><sup>FKL</sup> = (1 - M<sub>x</sub>) · Σ<sub>v∈V<sub>k</sub><sup>T</sup></sub> ∇<sub>θ</sub> log π<sub>S</sub>(v) · π<sub>T</sub>(v)
</div>

<p>Here, V<sub>k</sub><sup>T</sup> = TopK(π<sub>T</sub>) represents the teacher's top-k vocabulary tokens. Since the gradient is weighted by π<sub>T</sub>(v) instead of log(π<sub>T</sub>(x) / π<sub>S</sub>(x)), it cannot explode. This mode-covering objective acts like localized supervised finetuning, pulling the student's probability mass back toward the teacher's peaks.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">distribution-shift-fkd.canvas</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">visualizing RKL collapse vs Top-k FKL mode covering</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="chart-controls">
      <button class="chart-btn active" id="dist-opd-btn">Reverse KL (OPD)</button>
      <button class="chart-btn" id="dist-fkd-btn">Top-k Forward KL (TrOPD Outlier)</button>
      <button class="chart-btn" id="dist-step-btn" style="border-color: var(--cyan); color: var(--cyan); margin-left: auto;">Run Gradient Step</button>
    </div>
    <canvas id="dist-canvas" style="width: 100%; height: 200px; background: #0b0b0e; border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 6px;"></canvas>
  </div>
</div>

<p>If the student places zero probability on the teacher's top-k tokens (Σ<sub>v∈V<sub>k</sub><sup>T</sup></sub> π<sub>S</sub>(v) → 0), the Forward KL divergence goes to infinity. TrOPD handles this by restricting the summation only to tokens where π<sub>S</sub>(v) &gt; 0. This ensures that the auxiliary objective remains bounded and stable under all circumstances.</p>
</section>

<section id="s-guidance" data-code="off_policy">
<div class="section-no">04 — Off-Policy Guidance</div>
<h2>Off-Policy Prefix Guidance and Annealing</h2>
<p>On-policy reasoning optimization is heavily bottlenecked by the student's limited generation quality. When dealing with complex mathematical reasoning, a weak student policy may never generate a correct sequence of steps. Distilling purely from student rollouts (x ~ π<sub>S</sub>) forces the student to learn from its own low-quality trajectories, hindering optimization.</p>

<p>TrOPD solves this by introducing **Off-Policy Guidance**. During generation, we prefix the student's output with a history generated entirely by the teacher, and let the student complete the sequence:</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Hybrid Generation Layout</div>
  x = [x<sub>[:l]</sub> ~ π<sub>T</sub>, &nbsp;&nbsp; x<sub>[l:]</sub> ~ π<sub>S</sub>]
</div>

<p>For the teacher-generated prefix, the student performs imitation learning via forward KL. Initially, the prefix length l covers the entire training length. As training progresses, l is gradually annealed to zero using a cosine schedule:</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">prefix-annealing-slider</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">slide to simulate prefix length annealing over training steps</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div style="margin-bottom: 1.25rem;">
      <label for="anneal-slider" style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; font-family: var(--ff-mono); font-size: 0.75rem; color: var(--text2); margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
        <span>Training Step: <span id="anneal-step-label" style="color: var(--purple-l);">0</span> / 200</span>
        <span>Prefix Length Ratio: <span id="anneal-ratio-label" style="color: var(--cyan);">100%</span></span>
      </label>
      <input type="range" id="anneal-slider" min="0" max="200" value="0" style="width: 100%; accent-color: var(--purple); cursor: pointer;">
    </div>
    
    <div style="font-family: var(--ff-mono); font-size: 0.72rem; line-height: 1.6; border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 6px; padding: 0.75rem; background: var(--card2)">
      <div style="display: flex; gap: 0.25rem; flex-wrap: wrap;" id="anneal-tokens-wrapper">
        <!-- Rendered dynamically -->
      </div>
      <div style="margin-top: 0.75rem; display: flex; gap: 1rem; font-size: 0.65rem;">
        <span style="display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.25rem; color: var(--cyan);"><span style="width: 8px; height: 8px; border-radius: 50%; background: var(--cyan);"></span> Teacher-Generated Prefix (Off-Policy Imitation)</span>
        <span style="display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.25rem; color: var(--green);"><span style="width: 8px; height: 8px; border-radius: 50%; background: var(--green);"></span> Student-Generated Continuation (On-Policy Exploration)</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>By starting with 100% teacher-guided prefixes, the student starts training in high-quality regions. As the schedule anneals, the student is gradually introduced to its own errors, learning how to self-correct while remaining within the teacher's stable supervision envelope.</p>
</section>

<section id="s-results" data-code="results">
<div class="section-no">05 — Evaluation Results</div>
<h2>Empirical Payoff: Distillation Benchmarks</h2>
<p>TrOPD was evaluated across three distinct distillation settings, including single-domain (math) and multi-domain (math, code, science, instruction following) tasks, using **DeepSeek-Qwen2.5-1.5B** and **Qwen3-SFT-1.7B** as student models.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">distillation-performance-comparison</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">select model configurations to see benchmark gains</span>
  </div>
